    {
      "element_kind": "section",
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "model",
      "objective_ids": [
        "o1"
      ],
      "title": "Du, dere and De",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Modern Norwegian normally uses du to one person and dere to several people, even in many professional situations. In public-facing writing, du is unmarked and not impolite. The capitalized polite pronoun De exists, but it is much less common today and can sound very formal, distant or old-fashioned if the situation does not call for it. Do not switch between De and du in the same text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "If you choose the old formal system, keep the whole set consistent: subject "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "De",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", object "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Dem",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", and possessive "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "Deres",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ". In most modern emails, though, plain du/dere is the safer default."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "col_langs": [
